The code statements that lead to the bug are:
return rndis_filter_set_rss_param(rndis_dev, key);
Reason: The function netvsc_set_rxfh did not check whether the RX indirection table was allocated (ndc->rx_table_sz == 0). When the device has a single receive queue, the indirection table is not allocated, but this call proceeds to program the RSS hash key, causing a hang due to subsequent operations that assume the table exists.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!ndc->rx_table_sz)
    return -EOPNOTSUPP;
Reason: This check prevents RSS hash key programming when the device does not have an RX indirection table (i.e., rx_table_sz is zero, which occurs when only one receive queue is reported). By returning -EOPNOTSUPP early, the function rejects the update and avoids the hang, aligning with the device’s capabilities.

